.TH CP 1.SH NAMEcp \- copy files.SH SYNOPSIS\fBcp\fR[\-cnuv] file1 file2.br\fBcp\fR[\-cnuv] file ... directory.SH DESCRIPTION\fBcp\fRcopies one file to another, byte for byte, or several files into adirectory under their own names.  The names may contain wildcards; thedirectory may not..SH OPTIONS.TP 12\fB\-c\fRconfirm: ask\fBoverwrite \fIfile\fB?\fRbefore replacing a file that exists..TP 12\fB\-n\fRnoisy: print\fBcopying f1 to dir/f1\fRfor each file..TP 12\fB\-u\fR`upcase before compare', in its own words..TP 12\fB\-v\fRread back and verify the data written..SH EXAMPLES.nfcp notes notes.bak          one copycp -n a.c b.c src           two files into src, naming each.fi.SH NOTESNeeds Microware's\fBcio\fR.\fRRun with no arguments at all, it prints its usage and then stops with abus error; give it something to copy.  OS-9's own\fBcopy\fRand\fBdsave\fRare the other copiers..SH SEE ALSOmv, rm